  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "py101-md0",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "# Python 101 · Fundamentals\n\nWelcome to **Python** in Arima Notebooks! Each cell runs in a fresh Python 3 process — just write top-level code and press **Shift+Enter**.\n\n**Pre-injected Arima helpers** (available in every Python cell):\n- `barista.table(rows)` — print a list of dicts as a table\n- `barista.display(obj)` — pretty JSON\n- `barista.html(content)` — render inline HTML\n- `barista.stats(values)` — count / min / max / mean / std\n\n**Prerequisites:** none. **Need a library?** Install it from the **Packages → PyPI** tab.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py101-1",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "python",
      "source": "# The classic first program\nprint(\"Hello, World!\")\nprint(\"Welcome to Python in Arima Notebooks\")",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py101-md1",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## 1. Variables and types\n\nPython is dynamically typed — no type declarations. `type()` tells you a value's type.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py101-2",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "python",
      "source": "name = \"Arima\"        # str\nversion = 4           # int\npi = 3.14159          # float\nready = True          # bool\n\nfor value in (name, version, pi, ready):\n    print(f\"{value!r:>10}  ->  {type(value).__name__}\")",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py101-md2",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## 2. f-strings\n\nf-strings (`f\"...\"`) interpolate expressions inline — the cleanest way to format text.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py101-3",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "python",
      "source": "user = \"Sam\"\nitems = 3\nprice = 4.5\n\nprint(f\"{user} bought {items} coffees for ${items * price:.2f}\")\nprint(f\"Uppercase: {user.upper()} | length: {len(user)}\")",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py101-md3",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## 3. Control flow\n\n`if` / `elif` / `else`. Python uses **indentation** (4 spaces) instead of braces.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py101-4",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "python",
      "source": "def grade(score):\n    if score >= 90:\n        return \"A\"\n    elif score >= 80:\n        return \"B\"\n    elif score >= 70:\n        return \"C\"\n    else:\n        return \"F\"\n\nfor s in (95, 82, 71, 40):\n    print(f\"{s} -> {grade(s)}\")",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py101-md4",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## 4. Loops and comprehensions\n\n`for` iterates any sequence. **List comprehensions** build lists in one expressive line.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py101-5",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "python",
      "source": "squares = [n * n for n in range(1, 8)]\nprint(\"squares:\", squares)\n\nevens = [n for n in range(20) if n % 2 == 0]\nprint(\"evens:  \", evens)\n\ntotal = sum(squares)\nprint(\"sum of squares:\", total)",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py101-md5",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## 5. Functions\n\nFunctions use `def`. Parameters can have **defaults**; `*args` / `**kwargs` accept variable arguments.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py101-6",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "python",
      "source": "def greet(name, greeting=\"Hello\"):\n    return f\"{greeting}, {name}!\"\n\ndef total(*numbers):\n    return sum(numbers)\n\nprint(greet(\"Arima\"))\nprint(greet(\"Sam\", greeting=\"Hi\"))\nprint(\"total(1,2,3,4):\", total(1, 2, 3, 4))",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py101-md6",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## 6. Arima helpers\n\nUse `barista.table()` and `barista.stats()` for quick, readable output.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py101-7",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "python",
      "source": "people = [\n    {\"name\": \"Ada\", \"role\": \"Engineer\", \"age\": 36},\n    {\"name\": \"Linus\", \"role\": \"Kernel\", \"age\": 54},\n    {\"name\": \"Grace\", \"role\": \"Admiral\", \"age\": 85},\n]\nbarista.table(people)\n\nages = [p[\"age\"] for p in people]\nbarista.stats(ages)",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py101-md7",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## You did it! 🐍\n\nYou've covered Python's essentials. **Next:** *Python 201 · Collections & OOP* — lists, dicts, sets, classes, and error handling.\n\n> Tip: switch any cell to another language with the mode badge in its header, and install libraries from **Packages → PyPI**.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    }
  ]
}
